## MVC Comparison with ASP.NET

The project uses concepts that closely resemble ASP.NET MVC.

| ASP.NET MVC           | Spring Boot MVC             |
| --------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Model                 | `@Entity`                   |
| Controller            | `@Controller`               |
| Razor Views           | Thymeleaf                   |
| Entity Framework Core | JPA / Hibernate             |
| `DbSet<T>`            | `JpaRepository<T, ID>`      |
| Dependency Injection  | Spring Dependency Injection |
| `.csproj`             | `pom.xml`                   |
| `appsettings.json`    | `application.properties`    |
| EF Migrations         | Flyway                      |
| SQL Server / SQLite   | H2                          |

---

## Todo Model

The main domain model is the `Todo` entity.

```java
@Entity
public class Todo {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String title;

    private String description;

    private boolean completed;
}
```

This entity is mapped to the `todo` table in the database.

---

## Repository

Spring Data JPA handles the main CRUD database operations.

```java
public interface TodoRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Todo, Long> {
}
```

By extending `JpaRepository`, Spring automatically provides methods such as:

```text
findAll()
findById()
save()
deleteById()
```

---

## Service Layer

The service layer sits between the controller and repository.

```text
Controller
    |
    v
TodoService
    |
    v
TodoRepository
```

The service contains the application's business logic and database operations.

---

## Controller

The controller handles HTTP requests and routes them to the appropriate application logic.

Example routes include:

```text
GET     /
GET     /todos/create
POST    /todos
GET     /todos/{id}/edit
POST    /todos/{id}
POST    /todos/{id}/delete
```

---

## Views

Thymeleaf is used to generate the HTML views.

The main templates are:

```text
index.html
create.html
edit.html
```

For example:

```html
<tr th:each="todo : ${todos}">
    <td th:text="${todo.id}"></td>
    <td th:text="${todo.title}"></td>
    <td th:text="${todo.description}"></td>
</tr>
```

This is conceptually similar to using Razor:

```csharp
@foreach (var todo in Model)
{
    ...
}
```

---

## Database

The project uses H2 as a lightweight local SQL database.

The connection configuration is stored in:

```text
src/main/resources/application.properties
```

Example:

```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:file:./data/tododb
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
```

The database is stored locally inside:

```text
data/
```

---

## H2 Console

The H2 browser console is enabled.

After running the application, open:

```text
http://localhost:8080/h2-console
```

Use:

```text
JDBC URL: jdbc:h2:file:./data/tododb
Username: sa
Password:
```

Leave the password blank.

---

## Database Migrations

Flyway is used to manage schema migrations.

Migration files are stored in:

```text
src/main/resources/db/migration/
```

The first migration is:

```text
V1__create_todo_table.sql
```

Example:

```sql
CREATE TABLE todo (
    id BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    description VARCHAR(1000),
    completed BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE
);
```

Flyway records completed migrations in its own database table:

```text
flyway_schema_history
```

Future schema changes should be added as new migrations.

For example:

```text
V2__add_due_date.sql
V3__add_priority.sql
```

A migration that adds a due date could look like:

```sql
ALTER TABLE todo
ADD COLUMN due_date DATE;
```

---

## Application Configuration

The main application configuration is stored in:

```text
src/main/resources/application.properties
```

Example configuration:

```properties
spring.application.name=todoapp

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:file:./data/tododb
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=

spring.flyway.enabled=true
spring.flyway.locations=classpath:db/migration

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

spring.h2.console.enabled=true
spring.h2.console.path=/h2-console
```

---

## Running the Application

### Prerequisites

Make sure the following are installed:

```text
Java 21+
Maven
```

Check Java:

```powershell
java -version
```

Check Maven:

```powershell
mvn -version
```

---

## Run Using Maven

From the project folder:

```powershell
mvn spring-boot:run
```

Alternatively, use the Maven Wrapper:

```powershell
.\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
```

---

## Open the Application

Once the application starts, open:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

---

## Build the Project

Run:

```powershell
mvn clean package
```

The generated application JAR will appear inside:

```text
target/
```

You can then run it with:

```powershell
java -jar target/todoapp-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar
```

---

## CRUD Operations

The application demonstrates the four standard CRUD operations.

### Create

Create a new todo item.

```text
POST /todos
```

### Read

Display all todo items.

```text
GET /
```

### Update

Edit an existing todo.

```text
GET /todos/{id}/edit
POST /todos/{id}
```

### Delete

Delete an existing todo.

```text
POST /todos/{id}/delete
```

---

## CSS

Application styling is located at:

```text
src/main/resources/static/css/style.css
```

Spring Boot automatically serves static resources from:

```text
src/main/resources/static/
```

The stylesheet is referenced from Thymeleaf with:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" th:href="@{/css/style.css}">
```

